Subject: Run a compiled function that needs a record frame on a record, not a full activation

What this does
  - `JS_CallInternal` runs a compiled function that has no native entry, no control records and
    an ordinary calling convention through `aot_call_record`: the interpreter's own record frame
    (the same allocation and fill a record call does, in the frame arena) with the compiled body
    run on it. A throw or a return leaves to the C frame, a deopt resumes the threaded
    interpreter on the record, the record is released like any record, and the C stack
    margin is checked as JS_CallInternal does. A function with a try, a generator, a lazy
    function or anything else keeps the full activation.
  - A record entered this way carries `REGVM_FLAG_AOT_REC` and the C stack address it was
    entered at, so a backtrace orders it among the frameless compiled frames that live on the C
    stack.

Why we need it
  A compiled function that needs the record's tail (it reads `arguments`, the home object of a
  `super` call, or the new target) has no native entry, so every call to it from the interpreter
  or from C paid a stack frame, a copy of JS_CallInternal's prologue and js_regvm_execute's
  entry record: 24 percent of a class-heavy program, where the interpreter's record call runs
  the same functions without a C frame. Super calls and getters were 1.17x of the interpreter and
  are 1.02x.

